/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · literature batch y */
WB.L("dictionary-makers", "literature", "beginner", "The people who wrote the dictionary",
  "Somebody had to decide what every word means. Meet the stubborn, eccentric and occasionally imprisoned people who built the English dictionary.",
  [
    ["Before dictionaries", "For most of its history, English had no dictionary of everyday words at all.",
      "Shakespeare wrote his plays with no dictionary of ordinary English on his desk. That was normal. For centuries, English speakers simply used words and argued about them. The first English dictionaries appeared around 1600 and were modest. Robert Cawdrey's A Table Alphabeticall, published in 1604, listed only about 2,500 'hard words', mostly borrowed from Latin, French and Greek, for readers who found them puzzling.\n\nNobody thought ordinary words like 'dog' or 'run' needed defining. These early books were glossaries for the newly educated, not records of the whole language. The idea of capturing every word, common or rare, and how it was really used took another century and a half to arrive."],
    ["Johnson's dictionary", "Samuel Johnson built a landmark dictionary on evidence: quotations showing how good writers really used words.",
      "In 1746 a group of London booksellers hired Samuel Johnson, a brilliant but often broke writer, to produce a proper dictionary. The French Academy had taken decades with dozens of scholars. Johnson, with a handful of assistants in a house off Fleet Street, took about nine years. His Dictionary of the English Language appeared in 1755 with roughly 42,000 entries.\n\nIts real innovation was evidence. Johnson read widely, marked passages showing words in action and used more than 100,000 quotations to illustrate meanings. That method became the foundation of serious dictionary making in English. A few entries let his wit show, such as 'lexicographer: a writer of dictionaries; a harmless drudge', and his teasing definition of 'oats' as a grain 'which in England is generally given to horses, but in Scotland supports the people'."],
    ["Webster's American spelling", "Noah Webster used his dictionary to give the United States its own, simpler spelling.",
      "Across the Atlantic, the schoolteacher Noah Webster believed the new United States deserved its own version of English. He published An American Dictionary of the English Language in 1828 and used it to push spelling changes he thought simpler and more logical.\n\nThat is why Americans write 'color', 'center' and 'defense' while Australians, following British habits, write 'colour', 'centre' and 'defence'. Most of his more radical ideas never caught on, but his choices stuck in schools, newspapers and printing houses. One determined dictionary maker helped split English spelling into two traditions, which is why spellcheckers ask whether you want Australian or American English."],
    ["The Oxford English Dictionary", "The OED set out to trace the history of every English word, with help from an army of volunteer readers.",
      "In 1857 the Philological Society in London called for a dictionary recording every English word since around the 12th century and how its meaning had changed. Scottish schoolmaster James Murray became editor in 1879 and worked in a corrugated iron shed, the Scriptorium, lined with pigeonholes for slips of paper. The first part appeared in 1884, covering only 'A' to 'Ant'. Murray died in 1915. The dictionary was finished in 1928, in ten volumes, roughly 70 years after it was proposed.\n\nMurray appealed to the public to post in word examples on slips, and thousands of volunteers in Britain, America and Australia sent millions. One of the most productive was William Chester Minor, an American former army surgeon confined to Broadmoor, a hospital for the criminally insane, after killing a man in London in 1872."],
    ["Dictionaries today", "Modern dictionaries describe how people really speak rather than lay down the law.",
      "Australia got its own major dictionary, the Macquarie, in 1981, recording words like 'arvo', 'doona' and 'ute' with Australian meanings and pronunciations. That reflects a bigger change in thinking. Early dictionary makers often wanted to fix the language and decide what was correct. Most modern lexicographers are descriptive instead: they observe how people actually use words and record it, including slang and new coinages.\n\nWhen a dictionary adds a word, it is not approving it, only noting that enough people use it. Today editors search huge digital text collections to spot new words and shifting meanings, and online dictionaries are updated several times a year. Johnson's paper slips have become databases, but the goal is the same."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the first English dictionaries only list 'hard words'?", ["Printers could not fit many more words in a book","Ordinary words had not yet come into the language","Governments restricted books about everyday speech","Ordinary words seemed too obvious to need explaining"], 3, "Early dictionaries were glossaries for newly educated readers, not records of the whole language."],
    [1, "What was Johnson's key innovation in dictionary making?", ["Choosing the correct meaning of each word by committee","Listing words in order of how often they were spoken","Illustrating meanings with quotations from good writers","Copying the method of the French Academy word for word"], 2, "Evidence from real use became the foundation of serious dictionary making in English."],
    [2, "Why do Americans write 'color' while Australians write 'colour'?", ["Australian settlers chose it to differ from Britain","Webster promoted simpler spellings that stuck in the US","The two spellings were set by a treaty in 1828","Webster's reforms spread to every English-speaking country"], 1, "A determined dictionary maker helped split English spelling into two traditions."],
    [3, "How did the OED gather enough examples of English words?", ["Volunteer readers posted in millions of quotation slips","The editors read every book themselves in person","Computers searched huge digital libraries of text","Scholars at Oxford were each assigned one letter"], 0, "The OED depended on a community of readers, not only its editors."],
    [4, "What does it mean that modern dictionaries are 'descriptive'?", ["They decide which words are correct and ban the rest","They approve every single word that they include","They only include words found in classic literature","They record how people actually use words, slang included"], 3, "Adding a word records that enough people use it; it is not an endorsement."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Shakespeare had no dictionary to check. Who finally decided what every word means?"});

WB.L("australian-english", "literature", "beginner", "How Australian English was born",
  "Within a generation of 1788, visitors noticed that children in the colony sounded different. Here is how a new way of speaking English took shape.",
  [
    ["A new accent, quickly", "The Australian accent formed surprisingly quickly among the first colonial children.",
      "The First Fleet that arrived in 1788 carried people from all over Britain and Ireland: Londoners, rural English, Irish convicts, Scots and more. Their parents spoke a jumble of dialects. But children growing up together in Sydney did what children everywhere do: they copied each other more than their parents.\n\nBy the 1820s, visitors were already remarking that native born children, sometimes called currency lads and lasses, spoke differently. Linguists call this dialect levelling. When many accents mix, the strongest regional quirks tend to drop out and a new shared accent emerges. That is why Australian English is fairly uniform across a huge continent."],
    ["Words from Aboriginal languages", "Many everyday Australian words were borrowed from Aboriginal languages.",
      "When Captain Cook's expedition was stranded near what is now Cooktown in 1770, the crew recorded a word that sounded like 'kangaroo' from the local Guugu Yimithirr people. It became one of the most famous loanwords in the world. After 1788, settlers in Sydney borrowed more words from the Dharug language, including 'dingo', 'wombat' and 'koala'. 'Boomerang' also came from a Sydney area language, and 'billabong' is thought to come from Wiradjuri.\n\nHundreds of Australian place names, from Wagga Wagga to Kakadu, have Aboriginal origins too. Before colonisation there were around 250 Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages, many with several dialects. Many are now endangered, and communities are working to revive them."],
    ["Arvo, servo and brekkie", "Australians shorten words and add 'o' or 'ie', and it signals friendliness, not laziness.",
      "Ask an Aussie what they did this arvo and you might hear that they stopped at the servo, grabbed a sanga and caught up with a rello. Australian English is famous for diminutives: shortened words with an ending added. The two big endings are 'ie' or 'y', as in 'brekkie', 'barbie' and 'sunnies', and 'o', as in 'arvo', 'ambo', 'smoko' and 'bottle-o'. There are a great many of these forms.\n\nDespite the stereotype, they are not about laziness. Research suggests they often signal friendliness, informality and shared identity. Notice how 'ambo' sounds warmer than 'paramedic'."],
    ["Fair dinkum and accents", "Classic expressions often have surprising origins, and Australian accents vary along a spectrum more than by region.",
      "'Dinkum' appears in English regional speech in the 1800s, where it seems to have meant work or a fair share of work. In Australia it came to mean genuine, and 'fair dinkum' became a catchphrase. A popular story says it comes from Chinese miners saying 'ding kum' for real gold, but there is no good evidence for this.\n\nIn the 1960s, linguists Alexander Mitchell and Arthur Delbridge recorded thousands of school students and described three broad accent types. 'Broad' is the stereotypical outback sound, heard in the voice of Steve Irwin. 'Cultivated' is closer to British pronunciation and was once common among radio announcers. 'General', in between, is what most Australians speak. These are points on a scale, and cultivated has become much rarer."],
    ["Bathers, togs and myths", "Vocabulary differs by state, and the idea that Australians mumble to keep out flies is a myth.",
      "Accents are fairly uniform, but vocabulary is another story. What you wear to the beach depends on your state: 'bathers' is common in Victoria, South Australia and Western Australia, 'togs' in Queensland, and 'swimmers' or 'cossie' in New South Wales. A slice of fried potato in batter is a 'potato cake' in Melbourne and a 'potato scallop' in Sydney. Processed sandwich meat is 'devon', 'fritz', 'polony' or 'Belgium sausage' depending on where you grew up.\n\nAn old myth says Australians speak with half closed mouths to keep out flies, or that the accent comes from lazy or drunk convicts. Linguists reject this. Every accent is the result of speech drifting over time. Australian English sits alongside British, American and Indian English as a major variety. A humorous 1965 book, Let Stalk Strine, popularised the jokey name 'Strine'."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did a distinct Australian accent form so quickly?", ["Convicts deliberately invented a secret accent for privacy","Children copied each other, not their parents' dialects","The settlers brought one shared accent over from Britain","Aboriginal languages replaced English pronunciation"], 1, "Mixed accents level out when children grow up together, leaving a shared new accent."],
    [1, "Which Australian word came from the Guugu Yimithirr people?", ["Kangaroo","Dingo","Wombat","Billabong"], 0, "The word recorded near Cooktown in 1770 became one of the world's best-known loanwords."],
    [2, "What do words like 'arvo' and 'brekkie' usually signal?", ["Laziness and careless speech habits","A formal, official style of language","A lack of education among speakers","Friendliness, informality and identity"], 3, "Diminutives often mark a relaxed, warm relationship between speakers."],
    [3, "What is the likely truth about the origin of 'fair dinkum'?", ["It comes from a Chinese miners' phrase for real gold","It was invented by advertisers in the 1960s","It probably comes from a British dialect word","It comes from a Dharug word for honest"], 2, "The popular 'ding kum' story has no good evidence behind it."],
    [4, "What explains the 'half closed mouth' idea about the Australian accent?", ["It is true, and it began with the first convicts","It is a myth; the accent came from ordinary sound change","It is caused by the heat and flies of the climate","It was recorded by linguists in the 1960s"], 1, "Australian English follows the same rules of sound change as any other variety."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Within a generation of 1788, visitors noticed colonial children sounded different. Why?"});

WB.L("punctuation-history", "literature", "intermediate", "The secret history of punctuation",
  "Ancient Romans wrote without spaces, commas or full stops. The marks we now take for granted were invented one by one, often by monks and printers.",
  [
    ["Words without spaces", "Ancient Greek and Latin were usually written as one unbroken stream of letters.",
      "Imagine reading a book where every line looked like THISISHOWMOSTANCIENTTEXTSLOOKED. Much ancient Greek and Latin was written like this, which scholars call scriptio continua. There were no spaces between words, no lower case letters and very little punctuation. It worked because reading was usually done aloud, often by trained readers who prepared a text in advance, much as an actor studies a script.\n\nThe reader worked out where words and sentences broke by sounding them out. To us it seems maddening, but to a skilled Roman reader it was normal. Punctuation, as we know it, began as helpful notes for performers, telling them where to pause and breathe."],
    ["Dots, spaces and monks", "Dots at different heights marked pauses, and early medieval monks gave us spaces between words.",
      "Around 200 BCE, Aristophanes of Byzantium, who worked at the great library in Alexandria, is credited with a simple system. He placed dots at different heights beside the text. A low dot marked a short pause, a middle dot a slightly longer one and a high dot the end of a complete thought. The short section was a 'komma', Greek for a piece cut off, and the complete thought was a 'periodos', a full circuit. Those words became our comma and period. Many writers ignored the system.\n\nThe space between words may be the most important mark of all. From around the 7th century, Irish and Anglo-Saxon scribes copying Latin began leaving gaps, because Latin was foreign to them and spaces made it easier to read. Spaces also helped make silent reading more common."],
    ["The printers step in", "Venetian printers gave us the semicolon, and printing house style became many of our rules.",
      "When printing spread across Europe in the late 1400s, punctuation became more consistent, because thousands of copies came from the same set of type. One of the most influential printers was Aldus Manutius in Venice. In the 1490s his press printed a work by the scholar Pietro Bembo that used the semicolon, a mark that became popular across Europe. Aldus is also associated with italic type and with the elegant small books that were the paperbacks of their day.\n\nPrinters needed rules so that compositors setting type would make the same choices, and over time they settled on many conventions we still follow. Much of what we call correct punctuation began as a printing house's house style."],
    ["The apostrophe's troubled life", "The apostrophe arrived in English in the 1500s and has caused confusion ever since.",
      "The apostrophe came into English in the 16th century, borrowed from French and Italian printing, where it marked missing letters, as in 'don't' for 'do not'. Its use for possession, as in 'the dog's bone', developed gradually and was not fully settled until around the 1700s and 1800s.\n\nEven then, awkward exceptions remained, such as 'its' for possession and 'it's' for 'it is', which still trip up careful writers. The so-called greengrocer's apostrophe, as in 'banana's $3 a kilo', is a much mocked error, but it shows how slippery the rules are. Some place names have dropped the apostrophe altogether, and Australian naming authorities generally prefer place names without it, as in Kings Cross."],
    ["Failed marks and texting", "New marks spread only when many people adopt them at once, and texting is giving old marks new meanings.",
      "In 1962, American advertising executive Martin Speckter proposed the interrobang, a question mark and exclamation mark merged into one symbol, for startled questions such as 'You did what?' It had brief fame and appeared on some typewriters, but never entered everyday writing. Punctuation spreads only when many writers, printers and readers adopt it together, so the core marks have changed little in centuries.\n\nSend a friend a text that says 'Fine.' and they may worry you are upset. In casual messaging, the full stop at the end of a short message can look deliberate, and some research suggests readers find such messages less sincere. Exclamation marks often signal warmth. This is not decline. It is punctuation adapting to make meaning clear."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why could ancient texts be written without spaces or punctuation?", ["Ancient readers could not read very quickly","Spaces had not been invented as a concept in any language","Writers did not care whether anyone could read it","Reading was usually aloud, by trained readers"], 3, "Early punctuation began as notes to help performers know when to pause."],
    [1, "What was the purpose of the dots at different heights?", ["They separated words from each other","They showed which words were borrowed","They marked pauses of different lengths","They marked questions and exclamations"], 2, "The names 'komma' and 'periodos' became our comma and period."],
    [1, "What was the effect of adding spaces between words?", ["It made manuscripts longer and harder to copy","It made text easier to read and silent reading common","It made reading aloud more necessary","It had no effect until the invention of print"], 1, "Spaces meant readers no longer needed to sound out text to find the words."],
    [2, "Why did punctuation become more consistent after the late 1400s?", ["Printers needed fixed rules for identical copies","Governments passed laws about punctuation","Scribes were banned from copying books","Schools began teaching punctuation rules"], 0, "Many conventions we call correct began as printing house style."],
    [4, "Why have new punctuation marks like the interrobang rarely caught on?", ["Publishers refused to print them","They were too hard to type on keyboards","Readers prefer text without punctuation","Many writers must adopt them at once"], 3, "Marks succeed through widespread adoption, which is hard to arrange."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Ancient Romans read aloud for a reason. The marks we use today began as stage directions."});

WB.L("detective-fiction", "literature", "beginner", "How the detective story was invented",
  "From a murder in a Paris apartment to Sherlock Holmes and Agatha Christie, the whodunnit has a surprisingly short and lively history.",
  [
    ["Poe's locked room", "Edgar Allan Poe set the pattern for the detective story in 1841 with 'The Murders in the Rue Morgue'.",
      "In 1841, the American writer Edgar Allan Poe published 'The Murders in the Rue Morgue'. Two women are found brutally killed in a Paris apartment that seems impossible to enter or leave. The police are baffled. Then an eccentric amateur, C. Auguste Dupin, solves the case by pure reasoning, noticing clues everyone else missed. The answer is famously bizarre: an escaped orangutan. Poe wrote only three Dupin stories, but they set the pattern. There is a brilliant, slightly odd detective, a less clever friend who narrates, a puzzling crime and a final explanation that makes everything click. Almost every fictional detective since owes something to Dupin."],
    ["Why the 1800s?", "Detective fiction rose alongside modern police forces, crowded cities and a faith in careful observation.",
      "It is no accident that detective stories appeared in the 1800s. London's Metropolitan Police was founded in 1829, and in 1842 it set up a small detective branch. Big cities were full of strangers, and crime became something to investigate with evidence, not simply punish. Newspapers fed public fascination with real murder cases. At the same time, science was teaching people that careful observation could reveal hidden truths. The fictional detective captured that spirit. He, and later she, was a kind of scientist of crime, bringing order and reason to the chaos of the modern city. Readers found that deeply reassuring, and many still do."],
    ["Holmes and a Melbourne rival", "Sherlock Holmes became the world's most famous detective, yet a Melbourne novel outsold his first adventure.",
      "In 1887, a young doctor named Arthur Conan Doyle introduced Sherlock Holmes and Dr Watson in A Study in Scarlet. Holmes was partly modelled on Joseph Bell, an Edinburgh medical teacher who could deduce a patient's job from small details. Stories in The Strand Magazine from 1891 made him a sensation. When Doyle killed him off in 1893, readers cancelled their subscriptions, and Holmes returned in 1903.\n\nA year before Holmes appeared, Fergus Hume, a lawyer raised in New Zealand, published The Mystery of a Hansom Cab in Melbourne. It sold far more copies than Doyle's first Holmes novel, though Hume reportedly sold the rights cheaply and saw little profit."],
    ["Golden Age fair play", "Golden Age writers promised readers a fair chance to solve the crime before the detective did.",
      "Between the world wars, the puzzle mystery peaked. Agatha Christie's first novel, The Mysterious Affair at Styles, appeared in 1920 and introduced the Belgian detective Hercule Poirot. Dorothy L. Sayers, Margery Allingham and New Zealand's Ngaio Marsh were also leading writers. Their books often featured country houses, a closed group of suspects and a body in the library.\n\nThe central idea was fair play: every clue the detective used had to be shown to the reader too. In 1929 Ronald Knox published a tongue-in-cheek list of rules, including no supernatural forces and no undiscovered poisons. In 1930 many of these writers formed the Detection Club in London, promising to play fair."],
    ["Mean streets, modern crime", "American hardboiled writers took the detective out of the drawing room, and crime fiction has kept branching since.",
      "Not everyone loved tidy country house puzzles. In 1920s and 1930s America, Dashiell Hammett and Raymond Chandler created hardboiled detectives: tough, weary private investigators in corrupt cities like San Francisco and Los Angeles. Their stories were less about clever clues and more about violence, money and moral compromise. Chandler's Philip Marlowe became the model for countless film noir heroes. Since then, crime fiction has split into police procedurals, psychological thrillers, Scandinavian noir and rural Australian crime, from Arthur Upfield to Kerry Greenwood and Jane Harper. Yet the basic appeal remains. A crime throws the world out of balance, and a detective who notices what others miss puts it right."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What basic pattern did Poe's Dupin stories establish for detective fiction?", ["A police chief, a confession and a chase through the city streets","An odd genius, a narrating friend and a puzzle solved by reasoning","A secret society, a stolen treasure and a final duel at dawn","A grieving widow, a haunted house and a ghost who names the killer"], 1, "Poe's formula of a brilliant detective, a narrating friend and a clever solution still shapes the genre."],
    [1, "Why did detective fiction emerge in the 1800s?", ["Printing had only just been invented and publishers needed new stories","Crime had disappeared, so readers turned to imaginary cases","Governments paid authors to write stories that encouraged obedience","Cities, police and a faith in observation made crime-solving feel modern"], 3, "Crowded cities, new police detectives and respect for science made the reasoning detective a fitting hero."],
    [2, "What did the success of The Mystery of a Hansom Cab show?", ["A Melbourne crime novel could outsell Holmes's first adventure","Australian readers preferred stories set overseas","Crime fiction could only succeed through magazine serials","Holmes's first novel was an instant bestseller worldwide"], 0, "Hume's Melbourne-set novel was a huge success in Australia and Britain, outselling Doyle's first Holmes novel."],
    [3, "What did 'fair play' mean for Golden Age mystery writers?", ["The murderer always had to be caught within a single day","The detective had to be a police officer, never an amateur","Every clue the detective used had to be shown to the reader too","The victim had to be someone the reader disliked"], 2, "Fair play turned the mystery into a game in which a sharp reader could solve the case first."],
    [4, "How did hardboiled crime fiction differ from the country house puzzle?", ["It removed the detective and let the police solve everything","It focused on violence, money and moral compromise in corrupt cities","It was set entirely in small English villages","It gave readers every clue so they could solve the case first"], 1, "Hammett and Chandler swapped tidy puzzles for weary investigators in morally murky cities."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Where did the whodunnit begin? A Paris apartment, and a very odd culprit."});

WB.L("fairy-tale-origins", "literature", "beginner", "The dark origins of fairy tales",
  "Before Disney, Cinderella's stepsisters cut off parts of their feet to fit the slipper. Fairy tales have a long, strange and sometimes gruesome history.",
  [
    ["Older than writing", "Fairy tales began as spoken folk tales, and Cinderella-type stories have been told around the world for well over a thousand years.",
      "Long before anyone wrote them down, fairy tales were told aloud, by grandparents at the fire, by workers passing the time and by nurses to children. They were not just for kids. Adults told tales of clever peasants, cruel stepmothers and magical helpers to entertain each other and pass on lessons. Because the tales lived in memory, they changed with each teller and crossed borders. Folklorists catalogue thousands of tale types in the Aarne-Thompson-Uther index.\n\nCinderella is a good example. The Greek writer Strabo recorded the story of Rhodopis, whose sandal an eagle dropped in an Egyptian king's lap. A 9th-century Chinese tale tells of Ye Xian, helped by a fish's bones, who loses a golden shoe."],
    ["Perrault and Basile", "Charles Perrault turned folk tales into polished stories with morals for the French court.",
      "In 1697, Charles Perrault, a member of the French Academy, published a small book of tales that included 'Cinderella', 'Sleeping Beauty', 'Little Red Riding Hood' and 'Puss in Boots'. He polished folk tales for fashionable readers at the court of Louis XIV, adding wit and ending each tale with a little moral in verse. Perrault gave us the pumpkin coach and the glass slipper. His Red Riding Hood ends badly: the wolf eats her, and no woodcutter arrives. The moral warns young women about smooth-talking men. Earlier, in the 1630s, the Italian writer Giambattista Basile had published his own collection, including early versions of Cinderella and Sleeping Beauty."],
    ["The Brothers Grimm", "The Grimms collected German tales to preserve a national culture, but their sources were not simple peasants.",
      "In 1812, the German scholars Jacob and Wilhelm Grimm published the first volume of their Children's and Household Tales. They were language experts who wanted to preserve what they saw as the authentic voice of the German people, at a time when Germany was divided and occupied by Napoleon's France.\n\nTheir image of peasants telling ancient tales is a little misleading. Many storytellers were educated, middle-class young women, some of French Huguenot descent, who already knew Perrault's tales. One prized source, Dorothea Viehmann, was a tailor's widow with a vast memory for stories. The brothers revised the collection repeatedly, and the final edition of 1857 held around 200 tales."],
    ["Softer, but bloodier", "The Grimms removed sexual hints from later editions but kept the violence that punished wickedness.",
      "The first Grimm edition was not a children's book, and parents complained. So Wilhelm in particular reworked the tales for family reading. Sexual hints went. In the first 'Rapunzel', the witch discovers the prince's visits when Rapunzel innocently remarks that her clothes are getting tight around her waist. Later editions changed this to a harmless slip of the tongue. Evil mothers were often turned into stepmothers, to protect the ideal of motherhood.\n\nViolence mostly stayed, because it punished wickedness. In the Grimms' Cinderella, the stepsisters cut off a toe and a heel to fit the slipper, and at the wedding, birds peck out their eyes."],
    ["Andersen and endurance", "Andersen invented his own melancholy tales, and fairy tales endure because they handle big themes in simple shapes.",
      "Hans Christian Andersen mostly invented new tales, starting in 1835: 'The Little Mermaid', 'The Ugly Duckling', 'The Emperor's New Clothes' and 'The Snow Queen'. In his original 'Little Mermaid', the prince marries someone else. She refuses to kill him to save herself and dissolves into foam, becoming a spirit of the air. Many readers see Andersen's own loneliness in his stories.\n\nFairy tales keep returning because they deal with growing up, injustice, luck and courage. In 1976 Bruno Bettelheim argued that their frightening parts help children face fears safely, though his ideas are debated. A 2016 study suggested some tales may be thousands of years old, though such estimates are uncertain."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do very similar fairy tales appear in many different countries?", ["Every country copied them from one printed book","Travelling merchants sold the same pamphlets everywhere","Spoken tales changed with each teller and crossed borders","Each culture invented identical plots by pure coincidence"], 2, "Oral tales travelled with people and changed as they were retold, so related versions turn up worldwide."],
    [1, "What did Perrault typically add when he retold folk tales?", ["Wit, polish and a short moral in verse","Long descriptions of the tale's historical setting","Gruesome details to frighten children into obedience","Alternative endings chosen by the reader"], 0, "Perrault refined folk tales for the French court and closed each with a moral."],
    [2, "How did the Grimms' sources differ from the popular image?", ["They were all illiterate farmers who had never heard printed stories","They were professional actors from travelling theatres","They were children who made up the stories themselves","Many were educated young women who knew Perrault's tales"], 3, "The Grimms' storytellers often knew printed tales, so the 'pure peasant voice' is partly a myth."],
    [3, "What did later Grimm editions change, and what did they keep?", ["They removed the violence but kept the sexual content","They removed sexual hints but kept most of the violence","They kept both unchanged to stay true to the sources","They removed both and ended every tale happily"], 1, "Wilhelm cleaned up sexual content for families, but violence stayed because it punished wickedness."],
    [4, "What set Hans Christian Andersen's tales apart from the Grimms'?", ["He translated them word for word from Italian","He wrote only comic tales with cheerful endings","He collected them from German villagers","He mostly invented his own stories"], 3, "Andersen was an original author, and his tales were often melancholy."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Cinderella's story is older than you think, and the slipper wasn't always glass."});

WB.L("frankenstein", "literature", "intermediate", "Frankenstein and the birth of science fiction",
  "A teenager, a stormy summer and a ghost story contest produced one of the most influential novels ever written.",
  [
    ["The year without summer", "Frankenstein began in 1816, when a volcanic eruption's cold, stormy summer trapped young writers indoors.",
      "In April 1815, Mount Tambora in what is now Indonesia exploded in one of the largest eruptions in recorded history. Its ash and gases cooled the global climate, and Europe's 1816 became the year without a summer, with cold, rain and failed harvests. That June, a group of young English writers stayed near Lake Geneva: the poet Lord Byron at the Villa Diodati, and nearby Percy Bysshe Shelley, eighteen-year-old Mary Godwin, her stepsister Claire Clairmont and Byron's doctor, John Polidori. Trapped indoors, they read ghost stories aloud, and Byron proposed that each write one.\n\nMary struggled for days. Polidori's effort became The Vampyre in 1819, which helped shape the vampire as an elegant aristocrat."],
    ["A waking dream", "Mary Shelley, from a radical family, wrote Frankenstein before she was twenty-one after a vivid waking vision.",
      "After listening to Byron and Percy discuss whether life could be artificially created, Mary had what she later called a waking dream. She pictured a pale student kneeling beside a thing he had put together, watching it stir into life, then fleeing in horror. She described this in an introduction to the 1831 edition, so it is her own account, years later.\n\nMary was the daughter of two radical thinkers: Mary Wollstonecraft, author of A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, who died shortly after her birth, and the philosopher William Godwin. Frankenstein was published anonymously in January 1818, when she was twenty. Many reviewers assumed the author was a man."],
    ["Science at life's edge", "The novel drew on real experiments in which electricity made dead tissue move.",
      "Frankenstein was not pure fantasy. In the 1780s, the Italian scientist Luigi Galvani made the legs of dead frogs twitch using electricity, which led to the term galvanism. His nephew Giovanni Aldini went further. In London in 1803, he applied electric currents to the body of an executed criminal, making the face grimace and a hand rise, to the alarm of onlookers. Many people wondered whether electricity might be the spark of life itself. Mary Shelley knew about these debates. In the novel, Victor Frankenstein is deliberately vague about his methods, but a scientist using cutting-edge knowledge to cross a forbidden line came straight from the science of her day."],
    ["The creature speaks", "In the novel, Frankenstein is the scientist, and the creature is eloquent, lonely and rejected.",
      "Anyone who knows Frankenstein only from films may be surprised by the book. First, Frankenstein is the scientist, Victor. The creature is never given a name. Second, he is no grunting brute. Abandoned by his horrified creator, he teaches himself to speak and read by secretly watching a family through a gap in a cottage wall. He reads Milton's Paradise Lost and compares himself to both Adam and Satan. When he confronts Victor, he speaks with great eloquence about his loneliness and the cruelty he has suffered, and asks Victor to make him a companion. Victor's refusal sets off the tragic final events. The real horror of the book is rejection."],
    ["First science fiction?", "Frankenstein is often called the first science fiction novel because its monster comes from science, not magic.",
      "Frankenstein grew out of the Gothic tradition of haunted castles, which began with Horace Walpole's The Castle of Otranto in 1764. But Mary Shelley did something new. Her monster was made not by a curse or spell but by a scientist using knowledge and method. The writer Brian Aldiss argued this makes it the first true science fiction novel, a view many critics share. The subtitle, The Modern Prometheus, points to the myth of the Titan who stole fire from the gods. Her question was new: what responsibility do creators have for what they make?\n\nThe 1931 Universal film with Boris Karloff, and Jack Pierce's flat-topped makeup, turned the creature into a mostly silent icon."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What set the stage for the ghost story contest that produced Frankenstein?", ["A plague closed the Swiss borders and stranded them","A publisher offered a prize for the best horror story","A fire destroyed their lodgings and forced them to share one room","A volcanic eruption cooled the climate and kept the group indoors"], 3, "Tambora's eruption caused the cold, wet 1816 summer that trapped the writers indoors."],
    [1, "How did Mary Shelley later describe the origin of her story's central image?", ["As a true story told by a doctor she met in Geneva","As a waking dream of a student and his creature","As a newspaper report she had read years earlier","As a dream she had as a small child"], 1, "Her waking-dream account, written years later in 1831, is a famous but personal origin story."],
    [2, "Why did people in Mary Shelley's day find her premise believable?", ["Surgeons had already revived people after death","Chemists had created living cells in laboratories","Experiments with electricity had made dead tissue move","Doctors had built working mechanical limbs"], 2, "Galvani's and Aldini's electrical experiments made the idea of electricity as the spark of life seem plausible."],
    [3, "How does the creature in the novel differ from the film version?", ["He is eloquent and teaches himself to speak","He is a mindless brute who never learns to speak","He is a spirit that only the scientist can see","He is a cheerful helper who is welcomed by all"], 0, "The novel's creature is eloquent and lonely, so the real horror is rejection."],
    [4, "Why do many critics call Frankenstein the first science fiction novel?", ["Its monster is created through scientific method rather than magic","It was the first novel to be set in outer space","It predicted the exact technology used in modern laboratories","It was the first novel written by a woman"], 0, "By replacing curses with science, Shelley asked what creators owe to what they make."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The famous monster has no name, and he's far more articulate than the films let on."});

WB.L("children-learning-language", "literature", "advanced", "How children learn to talk",
  "By around age four, most children have mastered the basics of a staggeringly complex grammar without a single lesson. How do they do it?",
  [
    ["Listening before birth", "Babies tune in to language before birth, then narrow their hearing to the sounds that matter.",
      "Language learning starts earlier than most people think. In late pregnancy, a baby hears muffled sounds with their rhythm intact. Newborns prefer their mother's voice and the rhythm of the language they heard in the womb. Research comparing French and German babies suggests newborns' cries may carry traces of that melody.\n\nIn the first months, babies can tell apart speech sounds from almost any language, including contrasts their parents cannot hear. That fades. Research led by Janet Werker found that by around 10 to 12 months, babies lose sensitivity to differences their language does not use. This is specialisation, which makes learning words easier. It also helps explain why adults often struggle with sounds their own language lacks, such as Japanese speakers hearing the difference between English 'r' and 'l'."],
    ["Babbling to sentences", "Most children follow a broadly similar path from babbling to words to sentences, at their own pace.",
      "Around six or seven months, many babies begin babbling, repeating syllables like 'bababa' or 'dadada'. Deaf babies exposed to sign language babble with their hands in a similar rhythm, which suggests babbling is about language, not just mouth practice. First words usually arrive around the first birthday, though the range is wide. Words then come slowly. Often in the second year, many children go through a vocabulary burst, adding new words at a rapid pace. Two-word phrases like 'more milk' and 'daddy gone' appear, followed by longer sentences. By around four, most children speak in complex sentences. Every child is different, so these ages are general guides, not strict deadlines."],
    ["Mistakes that prove learning", "Errors like 'goed' and 'mouses' show children are working out rules, not just copying.",
      "One of the most charming signs of language learning is also one of the most revealing. A child who once said 'went' may suddenly say 'goed'. A child who said 'mice' may switch to 'mouses'. Parents sometimes worry, but this is progress. The child has discovered a rule, add '-ed' for the past or '-s' for plurals, and is applying it everywhere, even to exceptions. Linguists call this overregularisation. Nobody taught the child 'goed', and they almost certainly never heard an adult say it. They invented it from the pattern. Over time, children relearn the exceptions, and 'went' returns. The mistakes show that children are not just copying. They are building a system."],
    ["Born to speak?", "Scientists still debate how much language is built in, but children can even create new languages.",
      "In the 1950s and 1960s, Noam Chomsky argued that children could not learn language's complex rules from the limited, messy speech they hear, so humans must have an inborn capacity, sometimes called universal grammar. Michael Tomasello and others argue that general thinking skills, such as spotting patterns and reading intentions, plus huge practice, are enough. Most scientists agree humans have some special readiness, but disagree on how much.\n\nIn the late 1970s and 1980s, new schools in Nicaragua brought together deaf children who had lived isolated lives. In playgrounds and on buses they began signing, and a shared language formed. Later, younger children made it more systematic. Children can create and refine language."],
    ["Helping language grow", "Back-and-forth conversation, shared reading and play do more for young children's language than flashcards or apps.",
      "Research suggests what matters most for young children is back-and-forth conversation, not flashcards or apps. Responding to a baby's babbles, naming things in everyday routines, asking questions and waiting for answers all help. Reading together is especially valuable, because books contain words and sentence types that rarely come up in daily chat. Many people find the singsong voice adults use with babies, sometimes called parentese, a little silly, but studies suggest it helps babies pay attention to speech. Children raised with two or more languages are not confused by them, and bilingualism is common worldwide. This is general information. For milestones, see raisingchildren.net.au, and if you have concerns, talk to your GP, child health nurse or a speech pathologist."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do babies lose the ability to hear sound differences their language doesn't use?", ["Their brains specialise in the sounds that matter to them","Their ears slowly lose sensitivity to quiet sounds","They stop paying attention to unfamiliar voices","They forget sounds that are rarely spoken at home"], 0, "Narrowing is a specialisation: the brain commits to the sounds of the language around it."],
    [1, "What does the babbling of deaf babies exposed to sign language suggest?", ["Babbling has nothing to do with later speech","Deaf babies learn language faster than hearing babies","Babbling is about language, not just mouth practice","Babbling only develops when parents copy it"], 2, "Manual babbling shows babbling is a language skill that can take any form."],
    [2, "Why is a child saying 'goed' instead of 'went' a sign of progress?", ["The child is copying exactly what adults say","The child has learned a rule and applies it everywhere","The child has forgotten how to speak properly","The child is being careless about pronunciation"], 1, "Overregularisation shows children build a system of rules instead of simply copying."],
    [3, "What did the Nicaraguan deaf children show about language?", ["Children cannot learn a language without formal teaching","Sign languages are simpler than spoken languages","Older children learn language better than younger ones","Given the chance, children can create and refine a new language"], 3, "Nicaraguan Sign Language emerged among children and became more systematic with each younger group."],
    [4, "What helps young children's language most, according to research?", ["Educational apps played alone on a screen","Flashcards drilled daily from birth","Back-and-forth conversation and shared reading","Hearing only one carefully chosen language"], 2, "Responsive conversation, shared reading and play give children the practice language growth needs."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Children invent words they've never heard. What their mistakes reveal."});

WB.L("hero-journey-stories", "literature", "advanced", "The hidden shapes of stories",
  "Is there really only a handful of plots in the world? Writers and scientists have long tried to map the hidden structures beneath the stories we love.",
  [
    ["Early maps of plot", "Thinkers from Aristotle to Freytag described plot as a chain of causes that rises and falls.",
      "Think of almost any film you have enjoyed. Someone wants something, obstacles appear, things get worse, then there is a turning point and a resolution. It feels natural, but people have tried to describe the pattern for over two thousand years. In his Poetics, in the 4th century BCE, Aristotle observed that a good tragedy has a beginning, a middle and an end, and its events follow from one another by necessity or probability. A plot is a chain of causes, not a list of events.\n\nIn 1863 the German critic Gustav Freytag drew drama as a five-part pyramid: introduction, rising action, climax, falling action and catastrophe or resolution. It fits tragedies like Hamlet well, but suits many modern stories less well."],
    ["The hero's journey", "Joseph Campbell argued that hero myths around the world share one pattern, though critics say he picked examples that fit.",
      "In 1949 the American scholar Joseph Campbell published The Hero with a Thousand Faces. Drawing on myths from many cultures, he argued that hero stories often follow a similar path, which he called the monomyth. A hero leaves the ordinary world after a call to adventure, often helped by a mentor. The hero crosses into an unfamiliar world, faces trials and a great ordeal, gains a reward or insight, and returns home transformed. Campbell became enormously influential in Hollywood. George Lucas has said his work shaped Star Wars, and screenwriters later popularised the pattern as a practical template. Critics argue Campbell picked examples that fit and ignored many that do not, but the idea remains widely used."],
    ["Counting the plots", "Schemes that sort stories into a few types are useful for spotting patterns but can hide as much as they reveal.",
      "In 2004 the British journalist Christopher Booker published The Seven Basic Plots, arguing that stories fall into types such as overcoming the monster, rags to riches, the quest, comedy, tragedy and rebirth. Others propose anywhere from three to over thirty. Jaws and David and Goliath really are both about overcoming a monster. But critics say the categories are so broad that almost anything fits.\n\nKurt Vonnegut drew stories as lines on a graph, with good fortune up and bad fortune down. In 2016 US researchers analysed the emotional words in over 1,000 works of fiction and reported six basic emotional arcs. Like all such studies, it is open to debate."],
    ["Other storytelling traditions", "The conflict-driven Western plot is one model among many.",
      "Most of these models come from European and American traditions and assume stories are driven by conflict between a hero and an obstacle. Other traditions work differently. A classical structure from Chinese poetry, later used in Japanese and Korean storytelling and often called kishotenketsu in Japanese, has four parts: introduction, development, a twist and a conclusion that brings the parts together. The twist need not be a conflict, just a surprising new angle. Aboriginal storytelling traditions are often closely tied to land and place, and many stories connect to particular sites and to responsibilities for Country, rather than following one hero's arc. These differences remind us that the Western plot is one model among many."],
    ["Why structure matters", "Knowing story shapes helps readers notice craft and gives writers useful questions.",
      "Knowing these patterns does not mean every story must follow them. Many great books play with structure: they start at the end, tell events out of order, or refuse a tidy resolution. But understanding the common shapes is useful. As a reader, it helps you notice why a story feels satisfying or why it drags. As a writer, it gives you questions to ask: what does the main character want, what stands in the way, and what changes by the end? Psychologists have also found that people tend to remember information better when it is told as a story with causes and consequences. That may be why the same shapes keep appearing, from campfire tales to streaming series. We seem to think in stories."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was Aristotle's key point about plot?", ["A plot should contain as many surprises as possible","Events should follow from one another as a chain of causes","The ending of a story should always be a happy one","A plot should be built around a single hero's journey"], 1, "A plot is a chain of causes, not just a list of events."],
    [1, "What is a common criticism of Campbell's monomyth?", ["He chose examples that fit and ignored many that did not","He used only stories from modern Hollywood films","He claimed hero stories had no shared features","He argued every story must end in tragedy"], 0, "The hero's journey is a useful template, but it was built from selected examples."],
    [2, "Why do critics doubt schemes of 'seven basic plots'?", ["Nobody has ever managed to count the types of story","Every scheme agrees on the same number of plots","They only describe stories written before 1800","The categories are so broad that almost anything fits"], 3, "Broad categories reveal some patterns but can hide what makes individual stories different."],
    [3, "How does kishotenketsu differ from the Western conflict-driven arc?", ["It has no ending and leaves the story unresolved","It always features a single hero fighting an enemy","Its twist can be a new angle rather than a conflict","It consists of exactly seven stages of struggle"], 2, "Four-part structures show a story can be satisfying without conflict at its centre."],
    [4, "How can knowing story structure help a writer?", ["It guarantees that readers will enjoy the finished story","It suggests questions about what a character wants","It means every story should follow the same pattern","It removes the need for original ideas in a story"], 1, "Structure is a tool for asking good questions, not a rulebook that every story must obey."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Are there really only a handful of stories? A few thinkers say so."});

